# FinGuard AI Research

## Explainable Machine Learning for Credit Card Fraud Detection

### Abstract

Credit card fraud detection is challenging because fraudulent transactions represent a very small proportion of total transactions. This research investigates machine learning approaches for detecting fraudulent transactions under severe class imbalance.

Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost are compared using ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, Precision, Recall, and F1-score. Threshold optimization and SHAP-based Explainable AI are also investigated to understand model behavior and improve the transparency of fraud predictions.

### Research Question

How can machine learning and Explainable AI improve credit card fraud detection while balancing fraud detection and false-alarm rates?

## Objectives

- Compare Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost.
- Evaluate models using multiple fraud-detection metrics.
- Study the effect of classification thresholds.
- Use SHAP to explain model predictions.
- Analyze false positives and missed fraud cases.
- Identify a practical model for fraud detection.

## Dataset

The research uses the ULB Credit Card Fraud Detection dataset.

- Transactions: 284,807
- Fraudulent transactions: 492
- Legitimate transactions: 284,315
- Severe class imbalance

Because of the imbalance, ROC-AUC and PR-AUC are emphasized instead of accuracy alone.

